WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:02.090
- I went to law school, and I was like,

00:00:02.090 --> 00:00:04.100
"I'm gonna make it rain in the dough."

00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:06.811
And that's not happening at all.

00:00:06.811 --> 00:00:09.394
(upbeat music)

00:00:17.010 --> 00:00:18.820
That's a great question.

00:00:18.820 --> 00:00:20.770
- I think 'cause you
can flip them that way.

00:00:20.770 --> 00:00:22.700
- It's because it makes us
feel like fancy lawyers.

00:00:22.700 --> 00:00:24.550
Like, "Objection," flip.

00:00:24.550 --> 00:00:26.070
- And it's a little longer (chuckles).

00:00:26.070 --> 00:00:26.940
- Are they longer?

00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:28.970
You know what, they also don't have holes.

00:00:28.970 --> 00:00:30.350
- Yeah, they don't have holes in them.

00:00:30.350 --> 00:00:32.021
- Yeah, so our pens can't get stuck.

00:00:32.021 --> 00:00:34.604
(upbeat music)

00:00:35.580 --> 00:00:36.640
- 'Cause they're stressed out.

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:38.820
- I love my job, I really like what I do.

00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:40.310
- Yeah, I don't think everyone hates it.

00:00:40.310 --> 00:00:43.260
- No, maybe they should
stop being lawyers then,

00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:44.451
if they hate their job.

00:00:44.451 --> 00:00:47.710
(upbeat music)

00:00:47.710 --> 00:00:49.157
- Because they're stressed (laughs).

00:00:49.157 --> 00:00:51.630
- (chuckles) Super highly
stressed out people.

00:00:51.630 --> 00:00:52.490
- A lot of pressure.

00:00:52.490 --> 00:00:53.791
You're dealing with people's--

00:00:53.791 --> 00:00:56.290
Like the most important
part of people's lives.

00:00:56.290 --> 00:00:58.630
For some people, like the
biggest thing that's happened.

00:00:58.630 --> 00:01:01.210
- I defend people who are facing eviction,

00:01:01.210 --> 00:01:04.090
so my case, the outcome of it,

00:01:04.090 --> 00:01:06.470
will determine if a
person has a home or not.

00:01:06.470 --> 00:01:08.400
And that's a lot of pressure.

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:10.200
I drink a lot of juice.

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:11.730
- Tina does not like alcohol.

00:01:11.730 --> 00:01:12.969
- Well, let's not lie.

00:01:12.969 --> 00:01:15.886
(suspicious music)

00:01:17.450 --> 00:01:18.688
- Objection.

00:01:18.688 --> 00:01:21.260
- I've never once said objection.

00:01:21.260 --> 00:01:23.380
- You have to preserve
the objections, guys.

00:01:23.380 --> 00:01:24.480
- When are you objecting?

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:27.000
- Opposing does something
they're not supposed to do.

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:30.340
- How many lawyers are
really depos or trials?

00:01:30.340 --> 00:01:31.610
- And you've gotta preserve in the depo.

00:01:31.610 --> 00:01:34.070
- They only time I yell out, "Objection,"

00:01:34.070 --> 00:01:36.170
is when my boyfriend's talking nonsense.

00:01:36.170 --> 00:01:39.371
The only time I've ever
used that in my career.

00:01:39.371 --> 00:01:42.540
(upbeat music)

00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:46.170
I think people hate us
because a lot of lawyers

00:01:46.170 --> 00:01:50.070
on TV are super rich and smuggle money

00:01:50.070 --> 00:01:53.510
and they're like the ones that
defend the asbestos people.

00:01:53.510 --> 00:01:55.880
I'm gonna blame TV for the lawyer hatred.

00:01:55.880 --> 00:01:57.050
- Yeah, I don't know.

00:01:57.050 --> 00:01:59.220
I don't know where to pinpoint it.

00:01:59.220 --> 00:02:02.130
- TV.
(upbeat music)

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:05.370
I looked this one up.

00:02:05.370 --> 00:02:07.040
- You also Googled it?
- I did.

00:02:07.040 --> 00:02:09.580
I Googled this so hard because (laughs)

00:02:09.580 --> 00:02:11.460
my sister was running her mouth,

00:02:11.460 --> 00:02:13.180
and I was like, "Stop, I'm a doctor."

00:02:13.180 --> 00:02:14.510
And she goes, "No, you're not."

00:02:14.510 --> 00:02:16.340
And I looked it up,
and I was like, "Well."

00:02:16.340 --> 00:02:17.750
She's right and she's wrong.

00:02:17.750 --> 00:02:20.370
We have our JD, so we're Juris Doctors.

00:02:20.370 --> 00:02:22.980
But we're not allowed to
use it in the United States

00:02:22.980 --> 00:02:25.680
because it would present a misleading

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:28.690
type of role to the public by saying

00:02:28.690 --> 00:02:31.150
we have more education
than we actually do have.

00:02:31.150 --> 00:02:32.500
So, we wouldn't be the equivalent

00:02:32.500 --> 00:02:34.090
to like a medical doctor.

00:02:34.090 --> 00:02:37.150
And you can get your doctorate of law,

00:02:37.150 --> 00:02:38.610
which is like even higher.

00:02:38.610 --> 00:02:39.443
I looked into that

00:02:39.443 --> 00:02:41.210
because I wanted to be a forever student.

00:02:41.210 --> 00:02:42.050
And then, I was like,

00:02:42.050 --> 00:02:43.501
"No, homie, I'm not messing with that."

00:02:43.501 --> 00:02:46.760
(upbeat music)

00:02:46.760 --> 00:02:49.801
I object to this question.
(laughs)

00:02:49.801 --> 00:02:52.230
180% 'cause let me tell you.

00:02:52.230 --> 00:02:53.350
- I got a lot of debt.

00:02:53.350 --> 00:02:54.510
- So much debt.

00:02:54.510 --> 00:02:57.500
On my salary, I cannot afford my services.

00:02:57.500 --> 00:02:59.441
And it's not dope at all.

00:02:59.441 --> 00:03:02.500
(upbeat music)

00:03:02.500 --> 00:03:03.560
- My first year of law school,

00:03:03.560 --> 00:03:06.347
I was told, "Do not date another lawyer."

00:03:06.347 --> 00:03:07.550
And that's what I did.

00:03:07.550 --> 00:03:10.920
- This lawyer dates a
low voltage electrician.

00:03:10.920 --> 00:03:12.787
'Cause it has nothing to do with law.

00:03:12.787 --> 00:03:15.900
(upbeat music)

00:03:15.900 --> 00:03:17.490
- I majored in sociology.

00:03:17.490 --> 00:03:20.780
- I majored in criminal
justice and history.

00:03:20.780 --> 00:03:23.240
But a lot of lawyers
major in like philosophy,

00:03:23.240 --> 00:03:25.560
or English, or something like that, yeah.

00:03:25.560 --> 00:03:27.410
- It doesn't matter.
- Business.

00:03:27.410 --> 00:03:30.640
Engineers, surprisingly, do
really well on the bar exam.

00:03:30.640 --> 00:03:32.560
- Math majors too, do really well.

00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:34.027
- Anything, really, it doesn't matter.

00:03:34.027 --> 00:03:37.160
(upbeat music)

00:03:37.160 --> 00:03:39.060
- Lawyers can help people a lot.

00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:41.280
Like, they are a lot of
volunteer opportunities,

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:43.030
and pro bono opportunities.

00:03:43.030 --> 00:03:44.795
I don't know how many actually do it.

00:03:44.795 --> 00:03:45.730
- A good amount.

00:03:45.730 --> 00:03:47.560
- There's a lot of
potential there to help.

00:03:47.560 --> 00:03:48.850
- All I do is help.

00:03:48.850 --> 00:03:50.249
- That's nice (laughs).

00:03:50.249 --> 00:03:52.832
(upbeat music)

00:03:53.796 --> 00:03:55.960
I cannot count (laughs).

00:03:57.800 --> 00:03:59.660
- Counting to 10 today was a struggle.

00:03:59.660 --> 00:04:01.870
One of my clients came and said

00:04:01.870 --> 00:04:03.730
they had an illegal rent increase.

00:04:03.730 --> 00:04:08.210
So, I had to figure out the
math, and I just didn't.

00:04:08.210 --> 00:04:09.490
I had somebody else do it

00:04:09.490 --> 00:04:11.290
'cause I don't know what 3% of this.

00:04:12.220 --> 00:04:13.120
Like, I don't know that.

00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:15.000
- Lawyers don't like math.
- No.

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:16.326
- That's why we're lawyers.

00:04:16.326 --> 00:04:19.475
(upbeat music)

00:04:19.475 --> 00:04:21.126
- I think it's unethical for us to lie.

00:04:21.126 --> 00:04:21.959
- Yeah, and you're not supposed

00:04:21.959 --> 00:04:23.000
to lie.
- We can't lie.

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:26.070
- I think people think
we're like hiding evidence,

00:04:26.070 --> 00:04:28.420
and like throwing the gun in the ocean.

00:04:28.420 --> 00:04:29.580
And like, no.

00:04:29.580 --> 00:04:32.370
- That's because we watch
How To Get Away with Murder.

00:04:32.370 --> 00:04:34.850
Yeah, we can lose our license for lying.

00:04:34.850 --> 00:04:37.200
That's not cool, we do zealously advocate.

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:39.320
But no lies.

00:04:39.320 --> 00:04:41.903
(upbeat music)

00:04:43.250 --> 00:04:44.370
- Yes.
- We can.

00:04:44.370 --> 00:04:46.410
- Yes.
- We usually get kicked off.

00:04:46.410 --> 00:04:47.243
Have you ever served?

00:04:47.243 --> 00:04:49.530
- In San Francisco, I saw a
trial where they had like,

00:04:49.530 --> 00:04:51.210
not kidding, three lawyers on the jury.

00:04:51.210 --> 00:04:52.110
- No way.
- Yeah.

00:04:52.110 --> 00:04:53.540
- That's because San Francisco is dope.

00:04:53.540 --> 00:04:54.660
- It is.

00:04:54.660 --> 00:04:57.296
But yes, you can serve on jury duty.

00:04:57.296 --> 00:04:59.879
(upbeat music)

